fact_id,module,fact,source,page,quote,volatile,verified_by
M2-F001,M2,"All blood cells, including granulocytes, monocytes, dendritic cells (DCs), mast cells, lymphocytes and innate lymphoid cells (ILCs), come from one haematopoietic stem cell (HSC) in the bone marrow.",Abbas,30,"granulocytes, monocytes, DCs, mast cells, platelets, B and T lymphocytes, and ILCs all originate from a common HSC in the bone marrow",N,
M2-F002,M2,HSCs express CD34 and c-KIT and do not express lineage-specific markers.,Abbas,30,"HSCs can be identified by the presence of surface markers, including the proteins CD34 and c-KIT, and the absence of lineage-specific markers",N,
M2-F003,M2,"Common myeloid-megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors give rise to the granulocytic lineage (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and to monocytes.",Abbas,32,"give rise to committed precursors of the erythroid, megakaryocytic, granulocytic, and monocytic lineages",N,
M2-F004,M2,Most DCs arise from a precursor that also gives rise to monocytes.,Abbas,32,most DCs arise from a precursor that also gives rise to monocytes,N,
M2-F005,M2,"Mast cell progenitors arise from a common granulocyte/monocyte precursor, leave the bone marrow and mature in peripheral tissues.",Abbas,32,"Immature mast cell progenitors arise from a common granulocyte/monocyte precursor, leave the bone marrow, and mature into mast cells in peripheral tissues",N,
M2-F006,M2,"The common lymphoid progenitor gives rise to T cells, B cells and a shared precursor of natural killer (NK) cells and ILCs.",Abbas,30,The common lymphoid progenitor in the bone marrow that gives rise to T and B lymphocytes also gives rise to a precursor of both NK,N,
M2-F007,M2,G-CSF and GM-CSF stimulate neutrophil production.,Abbas,14,Production of neutrophils is stimulated by granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) and granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF),N,
M2-F008,M2,M-CSF drives the development of the monocyte-macrophage lineage from bone marrow precursors.,Abbas,15,driven by a cytokine called a monocyte (or macrophage) colony-stimulating factor (M-CSF),N,
M2-F009,M2,All DC subsets depend on FLT3 ligand (FLT3L) for development and express CD11c.,Abbas,20,"dependence on the cytokine FLT3L for their development, expression of the CD11c protein",N,
M2-F010,M2,Basophils mature in the bone marrow from progenitors that are different from mast cell progenitors.,Abbas,20,"mature in the bone marrow (from progenitors distinct from those of mast cells), and circulate in the blood",N,
M2-F011,M2,Janeway (8th ed.) states that DCs arise from both myeloid and lymphoid progenitors.,Janeway,77,Dendritic cells arise from both myeloid and lymphoid progenitors within the bone marrow,N,
M2-F012,M2,"Normal blood differential: neutrophils 40-60%, eosinophils 1-4%, basophils <1%, monocytes 2-8% of leukocytes.",Abbas,14,Neutrophils 4400 40%–60% Eosinophils 200 1%–4% Basophils 40 <1%,N,
M2-F013,M2,Neutrophils are the most abundant circulating white blood cells and the principal cell of acute inflammation.,Abbas,14,Neutrophils are the most abundant population of circulating white blood cells and the principal cell type in acute inflammatory reactions,N,
M2-F014,M2,"The neutrophil nucleus has three to five lobes, so neutrophils are also called polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMNs).",Abbas,14,The nucleus is segmented into three to five connected lobules,N,
M2-F015,M2,"Most neutrophil granules are specific granules, which contain lysozyme, collagenase and elastase.",Abbas,14,"called specific granules, are filled with enzymes, such as lysozyme, collagenase, and elastase",N,
M2-F016,M2,"Neutrophil azurophilic granules contain myeloperoxidase, defensins and cathelicidins.",Abbas,14,"azurophilic granules ... contain enzymes (e.g., myeloperoxidase) and microbicidal substances, including defensins and cathelicidins",N,
M2-F017,M2,"Human neutrophil surface markers include CD11b, CD18, CD15, CD66b and CD10.",Abbas,15,"Surface markers (human) CD11b, CD18, CD15, CD66b, CD10",N,
M2-F018,M2,"Neutrophils live 1-2 days in tissues, whereas tissue-resident macrophages can live for years.",Abbas,15,Life span in tissues 1–2 days ... Tissue-resident macrophages: years,N,
M2-F019,M2,Neutrophils respond rapidly by activating preformed enzymes; macrophage responses are slower and often need new gene transcription.,Abbas,15,"Rapid, short-lived, activation of preformed enzymes ... More prolonged, slower, often dependent on new gene transcription",N,
M2-F020,M2,Neutrophils can extrude nuclear contents as neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) that immobilize and kill microbes but can also damage healthy tissue.,Abbas,15,"extrude their nuclear contents, forming neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs), which serve to immobilize and kill extracellular microbes but also may damage healthy tissues",N,
M2-F021,M2,"Classical (inflammatory) monocytes form 90-95% of human blood monocytes; they are rapidly recruited to sites of infection or injury, where they differentiate into macrophages.",Abbas,15-16,"Blood monocytes are efficiently recruited into tissue sites of infection or injury, where they differentiate (mature) into macrophages",N,
M2-F022,M2,"Most tissue-resident macrophages are long-lived, self-renewing cells that derive from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors, not from bone marrow.",Abbas,15,Most tissue-resident macrophages are long-lived and are derived not from the bone marrow but from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors,N,
M2-F023,M2,"Examples of tissue-resident macrophages are Kupffer cells (liver), alveolar macrophages (lung) and microglia (brain).",Abbas,15,"Kupffer cells lining the sinusoids in the liver, alveolar macrophages in the lung, and microglial cells in the brain",N,
M2-F024,M2,Janeway (8th ed.) describes tissue macrophages as maturing continuously from blood monocytes.,Janeway,76,Macrophages mature continuously from monocytes that leave the circulation to migrate into tissues throughout the body,N,
M2-F025,M2,"Human macrophage markers include CD11b, CD14, CD68, CD16, CD64 and CCR5.",Abbas,15,"CD11b, CD14, CD68, CD16, CD64, CCR5",N,
M2-F026,M2,Macrophages remove apoptotic cells before they release their contents; this clearance is called efferocytosis.,Abbas,16,This clearance of apoptotic cells by macrophages is called efferocytosis,N,
M2-F027,M2,"The inhibitory receptor SIRPalpha on macrophages binds CD47 on healthy cells, a 'don't eat me' signal that blocks phagocytosis.",Abbas,19,"an inhibitory receptor on the macrophage called SIRPα, which recognizes CD47, a membrane protein on healthy cells that functions as a “don’t eat me” signal",N,
M2-F028,M2,Classical activation produces microbicidal M1 macrophages; alternative activation produces M2 macrophages that promote tissue remodeling and repair.,Abbas,19,"efficient at killing microbes, called classical activation, and these cells are often called M1 macrophages ... alternative activation, and these cells are called M2 macrophages",N,
M2-F029,M2,Macrophages are activated by microbial products such as lipopolysaccharide and by NK cell-derived interferon-gamma (IFN-gamma).,Abbas,19,Macrophages are activated by microbial products such as lipopolysaccharides and by natural killer cell–derived interferon-γ (IFN-γ),N,
M2-F030,M2,"In Abbas Fig. 2.4, cytokines such as IL-4 and IL-13 drive macrophage production of collagen and angiogenic factors, leading to tissue repair and fibrosis.",Abbas,19,"Cytokine (e.g., IL-4, IL-13) ... Production of collagen, angiogenic factors ... Tissue repair, fibrosis",N,
M2-F031,M2,Many macrophage subsets do not fall neatly into M1 or M2 categories.,Abbas,19,multiple macrophage subsets exist that do not neatly fall into M1 or M2 categories,N,
M2-F032,M2,"Activated macrophages produce cytokines such as TNF, IL-1, IL-6 and IL-12.",Abbas,19,"Cytokines (TNF, IL-1, IL-6, IL-12)",N,
M2-F033,M2,"DCs are sentinels in epithelia and tissues that capture antigens and carry them to lymph nodes, linking innate and adaptive immunity.",Abbas,20,their ability to capture antigens and take them to lymph nodes where naive T cells circulate ... link innate responses with the development of adaptive,N,
M2-F034,M2,cDC2 cells are the most numerous DCs and present antigens mainly to CD4+ T cells.,Abbas,21,cDC2 cells are the most numerous DCs and are potent at capturing exogenous antigens and inducing CD4+ T-cell responses,N,
M2-F035,M2,"cDC1 cells (IRF8, BATF3; CD141, CLEC9A, XCR1) cross-present antigens to naive CD8+ T cells and produce IL-12 to induce Th1 responses.",Abbas,21-23,The cDC1 subset is specialized to present antigens to naive CD8+ T cells by a process called cross-presentation,N,
M2-F036,M2,"Plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) express BDCA-2 (CD303) and CD123, have high levels of TLR7 and TLR9, use E2-2, and produce type I interferon (IFN).",Abbas,23,"BDCA-2 (CD303) BDCA-4 (CD304) CD123 ... High levels of TLR7, TLR9 ... Type I IFN",N,
M2-F037,M2,"pDCs are the body's major producers of type I IFNs, which have potent antiviral activity.",Abbas,22,"Plasmacytoid DCs are the body’s major producers of type I IFNs, which have potent antiviral activities",N,
M2-F038,M2,pDCs can make up to 1000 times more type I IFN than other cell types.,Janeway,111,make abundant type I interferons (IFN-α and IFN-β)—up to 1000 times more than other cell types,N,
M2-F039,M2,Langerhans cells are epidermal DCs with Birbeck granules and langerin (CD207); they derive from embryonic precursors related to tissue macrophages and present antigens to CD4+ T cells or induce tolerance.,Abbas,22,"Langerhans cells may function in the context of skin infections to present antigens to and activate CD4+ T cells, or ... induce tolerance",N,
M2-F040,M2,Follicular dendritic cells are stromal cells that are not bone marrow-derived and do not present protein antigens to T cells.,Abbas,22,"follicular dendritic cells (FDCs) have a dendritic morphology but are not derived from bone marrow precursors, do not present protein antigens to T cells",N,
M2-F041,M2,PAMP-activated DCs express costimulators and direct naive T-cell differentiation into effector subsets such as Th1 or Th17 cells.,Abbas,81,DCs activated by microbial PAMPs also express membrane molecules called costimulators ... DCs will direct naive T-cell differentiation into distinct types of effector cells,N,
M2-F042,M2,NK cells form 5-20% of lymphocytes in blood and spleen and are abundant in liver and placenta.,Abbas,82,NK cells constitute 5% to 20% of lymphocytes in the blood and spleen ... they are abundant in the liver and placenta,N,
M2-F043,M2,Blood NK cells are identified as CD56-positive and CD3-negative; most human blood NK cells also express CD16.,Abbas,82,identified in the blood by the expression of CD56 and the absence of the T-cell marker CD3. Most human blood NK cells also express CD16,N,
M2-F044,M2,"NK cells kill infected cells without prior activation, using germline-encoded receptors, not clonally distributed antigen receptors.",Abbas,82,NK cells are functionally competent to kill other cells ... without further activation (hence natural) ... use germline DNA–encoded receptors,N,
M2-F045,M2,"NK cells kill infected cells and secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to destroy phagocytosed microbes.",Abbas,82,"The effector functions of NK cells are to kill infected cells and to produce IFN-γ, which activates macrophages to destroy phagocytosed microbes",N,
M2-F046,M2,"In NK granules, perforin helps granzymes enter the target cell, and granzymes trigger apoptosis.",Abbas,82,"perforin, facilitates the entry of other granule proteins, called granzymes ... cause death of the target cells by apoptosis",N,
M2-F047,M2,"NK cells kill virus-infected cells early, before antigen-specific CTLs become fully active after 5-7 days.",Abbas,82,"they kill infected cells before antigen-specific CTLs can become fully active, which usually takes 5 to 7 days",N,
M2-F048,M2,"IL-12, IL-15, IL-18 and type I IFNs enhance NK cytotoxicity and IFN-gamma secretion; IL-15 is an NK growth factor.",Abbas,85,"The major cytokines of the innate immune system that stimulate NK cell function are IL-12, IL-15, IL-18, and type I IFNs",N,
M2-F049,M2,NK cell function depends on the balance between signals from activating and inhibitory receptors.,Abbas,83,NK cell function is regulated by a balance between signals that are generated by activating and inhibitory receptors,N,
M2-F050,M2,"NKG2D is an activating receptor that binds stress-induced MIC-A, MIC-B and ULBP proteins on infected and tumour cells and signals through DAP10.",Abbas,83,"NKG2D, which binds proteins structurally similar to MHC-I molecules, including MHC-I-related gene A (MIC-A) and MIC-B, and proteins of the ULBP ... DAP10",N,
M2-F051,M2,"Natural cytotoxicity receptors NKp30, NKp44 and NKp46 are activating NK receptors.",Janeway,117,"Activating receptors for the recognition of infected cells are the natural cytotoxicity receptors (NCRs) NKp30, NKp44, and NKp46",N,
M2-F052,M2,CD16 (FcgammaRIIIA) is a low-affinity IgG receptor that binds IgG1 and IgG3 and mediates antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC).,Abbas,84,"CD16 (FcγRIIIA), which is a low-affinity receptor for IgG antibodies ... binds to the Fc regions of certain types of antibodies called IgG1 and IgG3",N,
M2-F053,M2,"NK inhibitory receptors recognise MHC class I (MHC-I); viruses, cell stress and many cancers reduce MHC-I, and NK activation by cells that lack MHC-I is called recognition of missing self.",Abbas,85,many viruses and other causes of cell stress lead to a loss of cell surface expression of MHC-I ... has been called recognition of missing,N,
M2-F054,M2,"Inhibitory KIRs bind classical MHC-I molecules; the inhibitory CD94/NKG2A heterodimer binds HLA-E, which displays peptides from other MHC-I molecules.",Abbas,85,"CD94/NKG2A heterodimer, which recognizes a non-classical MHC-I molecule called HLA-E. Interestingly, HLA-E displays peptides derived from other MHC-I molecules",N,
M2-F055,M2,Inhibitory NK receptors carry ITIMs that recruit tyrosine phosphatases; activating receptors signal through ITAMs.,Abbas,85,Inhibitory receptors of NK cells have immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motifs (ITIMs) ... recruitment and activation of tyrosine phosphatases,N,
M2-F056,M2,"Inhibitory KIRs (KIR-2DL, KIR-3DL) have long cytoplasmic tails with ITIMs; activating KIRs (KIR-2DS, KIR-3DS) have short tails and pair with DAP12.",Janeway,115-116,Inhibitory KIRs have long cytoplasmic tails that contain an immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motif ... Activating KIR receptors have short cytoplasmic tails,N,
M2-F057,M2,NKG2A contains an ITIM and is inhibitory; NKG2C pairs with DAP12 and is activating.,Janeway,116,"NKG2A contains an ITIM and is inhibitory, whereas NKG2C has a charged transmembrane residue, associates with DAP12, and is activating",N,
M2-F058,M2,Interferons increase MHC-I on uninfected cells and so protect them from NK cells.,Janeway,115,"interferons, which induce the expression of MHC class I molecules, protect uninfected host cells from NK cells",N,
M2-F059,M2,Patients with numerical or functional NK cell deficiency present with severe herpesvirus and papillomavirus infections.,Abbas,488,"present with severe infections with viruses, mainly of the herpesvirus and papillomavirus families",N,
M2-F060,M2,"Autosomal dominant GATA2 mutations cause loss of NK cells together with reduced monocytes, DCs and B cells.",Abbas,488,"autosomal dominant mutations in the gene encoding the GATA2 transcription factor ... loss of NK cells, as well as decreases in monocytes, dendritic cells (DCs)",N,
M2-F061,M2,Autosomal recessive CD16 mutations cause a functional NK cell deficiency.,Abbas,488,One example of functional NK cell deficiency is autosomal recessive mutations affecting CD16 (FcγRIIIA),N,
M2-F062,M2,Chediak-Higashi syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder (LYST mutation) in which NK cell function is impaired because of abnormal cytotoxic granules.,Abbas,488,"Chédiak-Higashi syndrome (CHS) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder ... NK cell function in these patients is impaired, probably because of an abnormality in the",N,
M2-F063,M2,Mature mast cells are not found in the circulation; they lie near blood vessels and nerves and beneath epithelia.,Abbas,465,"Normally, mature mast cells are not found in the circulation ... predominantly near blood vessels (Fig. 20.2A) and nerves and beneath epithelia",N,
M2-F064,M2,Stem cell factor (SCF) binds c-KIT on mast cell precursors and is essential for mast cell development.,Abbas,465,"stem cell factor released by tissue cells, which binds to the c-KIT receptor on the mast cell precursors",N,
M2-F065,M2,Mast cells live weeks to months; basophils live days; eosinophils live days to weeks.,Abbas,466,Life span Weeks to months Days Days to weeks,N,
M2-F066,M2,Major growth factors: mast cells SCF and IL-3; basophils IL-3; eosinophils IL-5.,Abbas,466,"Major growth and differentiation factor (cytokines) Stem cell factor, IL-3 IL-3 IL-5",N,
M2-F067,M2,FcepsilonRI expression is high on mast cells and basophils and low on eosinophils.,Abbas,466,Expression of FcεRI High High Low,N,
M2-F068,M2,"FcepsilonRI binds IgE with very high affinity (Kd about 10^-10 M), so most mast cells are coated with IgE even in nonatopic people.",Abbas,466,"the majority of mast cells are always coated with IgE, even in nonatopic individuals",N,
M2-F069,M2,FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils is an alpha-beta-gamma2 tetramer; on eosinophils it is mainly an alpha-gamma2 trimer at low levels.,Abbas,467,"FcεRI on mast cells and basophils is expressed as an αβγ2 tetramer, the receptors on eosinophils are mainly αγ2 trimers and are expressed at low",N,
M2-F070,M2,Mast cells are activated when multivalent antigen cross-links IgE bound to FcepsilonRI.,Abbas,468,"Mast cells are activated by cross-linking of FcεRI molecules, which occurs by binding of multivalent antigens to the IgE molecules",N,
M2-F071,M2,"Mast cell activation causes degranulation, synthesis of lipid mediators and synthesis of cytokines.",Abbas,468,"secretion of preformed granule contents by exocytosis (degranulation), synthesis and secretion of lipid mediators, and synthesis and secretion of cytokines",N,
M2-F072,M2,"Mast cell and basophil granules store histamine, proteoglycans (heparin and/or chondroitin sulfate) and neutral proteases (tryptase and/or chymase).",Abbas,467,"Histamine ... Enzymes: neutral proteases (tryptase and/or chymase), acid hydrolases, cathepsin G, carboxypeptidase",N,
M2-F073,M2,"Mast cells and basophils make the lipid mediators prostaglandin D2 (PGD2), leukotrienes C4, D4, E4 and platelet-activating factor (PAF) on activation.",Abbas,467,"Major lipid mediators produced on activation PGD2 ... Leukotrienes C4, D4, E4 ... PAF",N,
M2-F074,M2,"Complement fragments C3a and C5a (anaphylatoxins) cause mast cell degranulation; TLR ligands, ATP and cytokines such as SCF and IL-33 can activate mast cells without IgE.",Abbas,470,"The complement fragments C3a and C5a can cause mast cell degranulation, and this is why they were named anaphylatoxins",N,
M2-F075,M2,"MRGPRX2 on skin mast cells binds some antibiotics, anaesthetic drugs and venom components, activates mast cells without IgE, and likely mediates some allergy-like drug reactions.",Abbas,470,"Ligands that bind to MRGPRX2 and thereby activate mast cells include several antibiotics and anesthetic drugs, components of insect venoms",N,
M2-F076,M2,The inhibitory receptor FcgammaRIIB (ITIM) co-aggregates with FcepsilonRI and inhibits mast cell signalling.,Abbas,470,"One such inhibitory receptor is FcγRIIB, which coaggregates with FcεRI during mast cell activation",N,
M2-F077,M2,"Tryptase is present in all human mast cells and in no other known cell type, so serum tryptase is a marker of mast cell activation used to diagnose anaphylaxis.",Abbas,471,Tryptase is present in all human mast cells and is not known to be present in any other cell type ... serum tryptase assays have,N,
M2-F078,M2,Human mast cell subsets contain tryptase only or tryptase and chymase.,Abbas,465,granule protease content (tryptase only or tryptase and chymase in humans),N,
M2-F079,M2,Systemic mastocytosis is a clonal mast cell disorder associated with c-KIT mutations and increased mediator release including tryptase.,Abbas,470-471,Systemic mastocytosis (SM) is a clinically heterogeneous disease associated with mutations in the tyrosine kinase c-KIT,N,
M2-F080,M2,Basophils are blood granulocytes (0.5% or less of leukocytes) that are normally absent from tissues but can be recruited to inflammatory sites.,Abbas,465,"Basophils constitute 0.5% or less of blood leukocytes. Although they are normally not present in tissues, basophils may be recruited",N,
M2-F081,M2,"Basophils express FcepsilonRI, bind IgE and can be triggered by antigen binding to IgE.",Abbas,465,"Like mast cells, basophils express Fcε receptor type I (FcεRI), bind IgE, and can be triggered by antigen binding to the IgE",N,
M2-F082,M2,"GM-CSF, IL-3 and IL-5 promote eosinophil maturation from myeloid precursors.",Abbas,20,"The cytokines GM-CSF, interleukin-3 (IL-3), and IL-5 promote eosinophil maturation from myeloid precursors",N,
M2-F083,M2,"Eosinophils carry Fc receptors for IgA and IgG, TLRs and IL-5 receptors that can trigger granule release.",Abbas,20,"including Fc receptors for IgA and IgG, TLRs, and IL-5 receptors, can generate signals that activate the cells to release their granule contents",N,
M2-F084,M2,"Eosinophils normally reside in the mucosa of the respiratory, gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts.",Abbas,20,"especially in mucosal linings of the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary tracts",N,
M2-F085,M2,"Eosinophil granules contain major basic protein (MBP) and eosinophil cationic protein (ECP), which damage helminths, bacteria and host cells.",Abbas,473,"major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein ... damage the integument of helminths and the cell walls of bacteria, as well as cells in normal",N,
M2-F086,M2,Eosinophil peroxidase is distinct from neutrophil myeloperoxidase and makes hypochlorous or hypobromous acid.,Abbas,473,"eosinophil peroxidase, which is distinct from the myeloperoxidase found in neutrophils and catalyzes the production of hypochlorous or hypobromous acid",N,
M2-F087,M2,"Eosinophils produce large amounts of galectin-10, which forms Charcot-Leyden crystals at sites of chronic type 2 inflammation such as asthmatic bronchi.",Abbas,473,Eosinophils produce large amounts of the protein galectin-10 ... Charcot-Leyden crystals ... such as the bronchi of allergic asthma patients,N,
M2-F088,M2,"Eotaxin (CCL11) binds CCR3 on eosinophils; VCAM-1/VLA-4, C5a, PAF and LTB4 also recruit eosinophils.",Abbas,473,eotaxin (CCL11) ... binds to the chemokine receptor CCR3 expressed on eosinophils,N,
M2-F089,M2,IL-5 from Th2 cells and ILC2s increases eosinophil production in bone marrow and activates mature eosinophils.,Abbas,473,"Both Th2 cells and ILC2s are sources of IL-5, which stimulates bone marrow production of eosinophils and is a potent activator of mature eosinophils",N,
M2-F090,M2,Eosinophils express very low FcepsilonRI that lacks a signalling chain; there is no convincing evidence that IgE-antigen activates them.,Abbas,473,"eosinophils express very low levels of FcεRI, and the receptor lacks a signaling chain, so its function in these cells is unclear",N,
M2-F091,M2,"Neutrophils, Th2 cells and eosinophils are recruited in the late-phase reaction, a few hours to 2 days after allergen exposure.",Abbas,465,"late-phase reactions, which occur a few hours to two days after allergen exposure",N,
M2-F092,M2,ILCs are bone marrow-derived lymphoid cells without T-cell receptors that secrete helper-T-like cytokines and live mainly in mucosal tissues; they are rare in blood.,Abbas,30,"ILCs are rare in the blood and are present mostly in tissues, especially mucosal tissues such as the lung and intestines",N,
M2-F093,M2,A common ILC precursor is identified by the transcription factor Id2.,Abbas,81,A common ILC precursor identified by the Id2 transcription factor differentiates into three major subsets,N,
M2-F094,M2,ILC1s make IFN-gamma (T-BET); ILC2s make mainly IL-5 and IL-13 (GATA3); ILC3s make IL-22 and/or IL-17 (RORgammat).,Abbas,81,ILC1s produce IFN-γ and express the transcription factor T-BET ... ILC2s produce mainly IL-5 and IL-13 and express ... GATA3 ... ILC3s produce IL-22 and/or,N,
M2-F095,M2,"ILC subsets are activated by cytokines: ILC1 by IL-12, IL-15, IL-18; ILC2 by IL-25, IL-33, TSLP; ILC3 by IL-1, IL-23.",Abbas,81,each ILC subset is activated by different cytokines (see Fig. 4.8),N,
M2-F096,M2,ILC1s defend against intracellular microbes; ILC2s against helminths and contribute to allergy; ILC3s defend against extracellular fungi and bacteria and maintain epithelial barriers.,Abbas,81-82,ILC2s may play a role in defense against helminthic parasites and also contribute to allergic diseases,N,
M2-F097,M2,"NK cells resemble ILC1s because they secrete IFN-gamma, but ILC1s are not cytotoxic.",Abbas,82,They are developmentally related to ILC1s but are not considered identical because ILC1s do not have cytotoxic functions,N,
M2-F098,M2,"Epithelial cells release IL-25, IL-33 and TSLP, which stimulate ILC2s, Th2 cells and mast cells to produce IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13; IL-33 is an alarmin stored in epithelial nuclei.",Abbas,94,"IL-25, thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP), and IL-33 ... stimulate ILC2s, Th2 cells, and mast cells to produce IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13",N,
M2-F099,M2,"Because ILC2s reside in tissues, they may drive early allergic inflammation before Th2 cells arrive and may later sustain it with Th2 cells.",Abbas,465,their cytokines may contribute to early allergic inflammation before Th2 cells are generated and migrate to the tissues,N,
M2-F100,M2,"iNKT cells, gammadelta T cells, MAIT cells, B-1 cells and marginal-zone B cells have antigen receptors of limited diversity and behave more like innate cells.",Abbas,85-86,"T cells with limited antigen receptor diversity include invariant natural killer T (iNKT) cells, γδ T cells, mucosa-associated invariant T (MAIT) cells",N,
M2-F101,M2,MAIT cells recognise bacterial riboflavin metabolites and make up almost 50% of liver lymphocytes.,Abbas,24,"Limited specificity for bacterial-derived riboflavin metabolites ... In the liver, almost 50% of the lymphocytes are MAIT cells",N,
M2-F102,M2,"iNKT cells recognise glycolipids presented by CD1 and rapidly secrete IL-4, IL-10 and IFN-gamma.",Janeway,119,"able to recognize glycolipid antigens presented to them by the MHC-like molecule CD1 ... rapid secretion of cytokines, including IL-4, IL-10, and IFN-γ",N,
M2-F103,M2,Intraepithelial gammadelta T cells recognise target molecules directly rather than peptide-MHC complexes.,Janeway,119,"γ:δ T cells do not generally recognize antigen as peptides presented by MHC molecules; instead, they seem to recognize their target antigens directly",N,
M2-F104,M2,B-1 cells make IgM against polysaccharides without T-cell help within 48 hours; in mice they protect against Streptococcus pneumoniae.,Janeway,119,can produce antibodies of the IgM class without ‘help’ from T cells ... they first appear within 48 hours of exposure to antigen,N,
M2-F105,M2,"Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is caused by defective phagocyte oxidase and production of reactive oxygen species, with recurrent bacterial and fungal infections.",Abbas,487,Chronic granulomatous disease Defective production of reactive oxygen species by phagocytes; recurrent bacterial and fungal infections,N,
M2-F106,M2,"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (LAD-1) is caused by mutations in CD18, the beta chain of beta2 integrins.",Abbas,487,Mutations in gene encoding the β chain (CD18) of β2 integrins,N,
M2-F107,M2,"Mast cells, basophils and eosinophils are myeloid cells with granules full of inflammatory mediators; all three take part in defence against helminths and in allergic disease.",Abbas,20,All three share the common property of having cytoplasmic granules filled with various inflammatory and antimicrobial mediators,N,
M2-F108,M2,FcepsilonRII (CD23) is a C-type lectin-like IgE receptor with much lower affinity than FcepsilonRI; its biologic role is not known.,Abbas,467,"Another IgE receptor called FcεRII, also known as CD23, is a protein related to C-type mammalian lectins whose affinity for IgE is much lower",N,
